@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer
@implements IAsyncDisposable

@* FR-119: the recipient signs on the device, and the drawing becomes one of the proof's assets.

   A <canvas> the module draws into, for the reason DtMap's container is an empty <div>: the pixels
   are outside Blazor's diff, so anything the renderer wrote here would be wiped on the first stroke.
   The element is sized in CSS and its backing store is sized by the module at first render, because
   a canvas whose attribute size and CSS size disagree draws a signature that does not follow the
   finger.

   The design gives the pad three states and this markup is the whole of them. Empty is a dashed
   frame with a baseline, a cross and the sentence saying what to do with it - a driver at a door
   has to be able to tell at a glance that this box is somewhere to draw rather than somewhere
   to read. Signed is the same frame gone solid with the invitation withdrawn and a hint saying the
   stroke registered. Cleared is empty again, which is why both arms read one field rather than two.

   That field is fed from the browser, not guessed at here: the module owns `marked` - it is the
   same flag `read()` answers null on - and a second copy of it in C# would be a copy free to
   disagree with the bytes actually on the canvas. So the module calls NotifyMarkedAsync on the
   first stroke and ClearAsync reads the flag back rather than assuming what clearing did.

   Every JS call happens in OnAfterRenderAsync or later, so static rendering never reaches IJSRuntime
   and this component renders on the server's first pass exactly as it does in a render test - in the
   empty state, which is the state a freshly opened pad is in. *@

<div class="dt-signature">
    @* The frame, and everything drawn inside it. The decorations come before the canvas so the
       canvas paints over them, and they take no pointer events of their own - a finger that came
       down on the prompt rather than on bare canvas must still start a stroke. *@
    <div class="dt-signature-pad @SignedClass">
        @if (!_signed)
        {
            <span class="dt-signature-baseline" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="dt-signature-mark" aria-hidden="true"><Icon Name="IconName.Close" /></span>
            <span class="dt-signature-prompt dt-type-small">@Localizer["SignaturePrompt"]</span>
        }

        <canvas @ref="_element" class="dt-signature-canvas" role="img"
                aria-label="@Localizer["SignatureLabel"]"></canvas>
    </div>

    @* Clear and the state hint on one line under the pad, which is where the design puts them: the
       hint qualifies the pad and the action undoes it, and a driver reads both with the same
       glance. role="status" so the change from empty to signed is announced rather than only
       drawn - the canvas itself cannot say it, because its accessible name never changes. *@
    <div class="dt-signature-foot">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm dt-signature-clear" @onclick="ClearAsync">
            <Icon Name="IconName.Close" />@Localizer["SignatureClear"]
        </button>

        @* One region, rendered from the first pass and never replaced - only its text changes. A
           live region inserted into the page together with its content is routinely not announced,
           because the observer has nothing to compare the insertion against, and two @@if arms each
           carrying their own role="status" is exactly that: every change destroys one region and
           creates another. The arms are therefore inside it rather than around it.

           Independent @@if blocks rather than an if/else chain, for the reason every other screen in
           this product writes them that way: an `else` reads to NFR-14's source scan as a rendered
           English word, and a key chosen in C# is a key the closed-catalogue test cannot see. *@
        <span class="dt-signature-state dt-type-small @StateClass" role="status">
            @if (_signed)
            {
                <Icon Name="IconName.Confirm" />@Localizer["SignatureSigned"]
            }
            @if (!_signed)
            {
                @Localizer["SignatureEmpty"]
            }
        </span>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private const string ModulePath = "./Components/Shared/DtSignaturePad.razor.js";

    private ElementReference _element;
    private IJSObjectReference? _module;
    private DotNetObjectReference<DtSignaturePad>? _self;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether anything has been drawn, as the module last reported it. Never set from a guess: the
    /// only two writers are <see cref="NotifyMarkedAsync"/>, which the module calls on the first
    /// stroke, and <see cref="ClearAsync"/>, which reads the flag back out of the module afterwards.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _signed;

    /// <summary>The modifier this pad's frame is wearing.</summary>
    private string SignedClass => Modifier(_signed);

    /// <summary>
    /// The modifier the state hint is wearing. A class on the one stable region rather than a second
    /// region: changing an element's class does not replace the element, and replacing it is what
    /// costs the announcement.
    /// </summary>
    private string StateClass => _signed ? "dt-signature-state--signed" : string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// The modifier a frame wears in each of the pad's two states: solid once there is a stroke on
    /// it, and nothing while it is still empty and dashed.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Internal and static because the signed half is unreachable from a render test: the harness
    /// renders statically, dispatches no pointer events and executes no module, so a pad it draws is
    /// always the empty one. Written the other way round - the modifier on the empty state and none
    /// on the signed one - every assertion this suite can make about the rendered pad still holds,
    /// while a driver is shown a solid frame inviting nothing and a dashed one over a signature that
    /// was already given.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="signed">Whether anything has been drawn.</param>
    internal static string Modifier(bool signed) =>
        signed ? "dt-signature-pad--signed" : string.Empty;

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender)
        {
            return;
        }

        // Imported at first render rather than at initialisation: the element the module attaches
        // its pointer listeners to does not exist until the markup above has been written.
        _module = await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", ModulePath);
        _self ??= DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);

        await _module.InvokeVoidAsync("attach", _element, _self);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Called by the module the first time a pointer comes down on the canvas, and never again for
    /// the same stroke run.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A push rather than a poll, because the thing being reported happens in the browser and
    /// nothing on the server would otherwise ask. It is one message per pad per signature - the
    /// module reports the transition, not the movement - so a signature drawn quickly does not
    /// become a stream of interop calls up the circuit.
    /// <para>
    /// The flag is read back rather than assigned true, for the same reason <see cref="ClearAsync"/>
    /// reads it back: this message crosses a circuit and can still be in flight when a wipe
    /// completes. Assigning true on arrival would leave the pad saying "signed" over a canvas that
    /// has just been cleared - and the capture would then send no signature at all, which is the one
    /// state this hint exists to keep a driver out of.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    [JSInvokable]
    public async Task NotifyMarkedAsync()
    {
        if (_module is null)
        {
            return;
        }

        _signed = await _module.InvokeAsync<bool>("marked", _element);

        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The drawing as base64-encoded PNG, or null when nothing has been drawn.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Base64 rather than a stream, because this is the one place the bytes exist in a browser and
    /// the only way back from one is a string. The screen decodes it once and hands the capability a
    /// <c>MemoryStream</c>, so nothing below the component ever sees an encoding.
    /// <para>
    /// Null for an untouched pad is what lets the screen refuse before a round trip; the validator
    /// refuses the same absence afterwards, which is the decision that actually counts.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public async Task<string?> ReadPngBase64Async()
    {
        if (_module is null)
        {
            return null;
        }

        return await _module.InvokeAsync<string?>("read", _element);
    }

    /// <summary>Wipes the pad, so a mis-drawn signature is redrawn rather than submitted.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The rendered state is read back out of the module rather than assigned false here. The module
    /// owns <c>marked</c> - it is the flag <c>read()</c> answers null on - and a pad that said
    /// "empty" while the module still held a stroke would be a frame inviting a signature over a
    /// signature. The extra call is one round trip on a button press, which is not a path anything
    /// is waiting on.
    /// </remarks>
    public async Task ClearAsync()
    {
        if (_module is null)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            await _module.InvokeVoidAsync("clear", _element);

            _signed = await _module.InvokeAsync<bool>("marked", _element);
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // Guarded like teardown is, and for a sharper reason: this runs from a click. The
            // circuit can drop while the dialog is open, and an exception out of an event handler is
            // not a failed wipe - it is an unhandled error that takes the screen down, losing the
            // recipient's name and the signature along with the pad nobody managed to clear.
        }
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            if (_module is null)
            {
                return;
            }

            await _module.InvokeVoidAsync("detach", _element);
            await _module.DisposeAsync();
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // The circuit went away before the component did. There is no browser left to tidy,
            // and throwing here would replace a disconnect with an unhandled error.
        }
        finally
        {
            // In the finally, so "whether or not" is true of every exception and of the early return
            // as well, rather than only of the one caught above. The reference is this side's and
            // nothing else releases it: a detach that threw anything else would otherwise leak one
            // per dialog opened.
            _self?.Dispose();
        }
    }
}
